{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## `Verified -1` on patchset 2 — a real failure, and a guard from a parallel workstream\n\nNot environmental, and not rechecked. Three cells (py3.11/3.12/3.13) failed identically:\n\n    FAILED tests/unit/test_alarm_window_tuning.py::test_breaching_alarms_require_every_period_in_the_window\n    monitoring_9313.tf:observability_probe_truncated      period=300 evaluation_periods=3 datapoints_to_alarm=2 breaching\n    monitoring_9313.tf:observability_probe_not_completing period=300 evaluation_periods=3 datapoints_to_alarm=2 breaching\n\nTicket `a9d1-c7f3-cfd9-44ff` (`illbred-sour-alpaca`) merged while this was in review and landed\nthat invariant plus `infra/runbooks/alarm-window-tuning.md`. **I1:** a `breaching` alarm must have\n`datapoints_to_alarm == evaluation_periods`, because under `M < N` missing buckets can OUT-VOTE a\nreal datapoint — a gap pages on its own, and a slow publisher holds the alarm in ALARM\n*unclearably*. Measured: 10.5 hours stuck on `rebar-docker-buildkit-cache-high`, and four\nsimultaneous false alarms at 20:47Z against verified-healthy infrastructure.\n\nMy alarms were authored at `M=2 of N=3` before that test existed. The guard caught a real\nviolation in my change. That is the second time in this ticket a structural check has caught\nsomething no behavioural test could see, and I note that the alarm workstream and I arrived at the\nsame conclusion from opposite ends — they measured the gaps my probe was producing, I measured the\ntruncation producing them. The runbook names `probe_ok`/`probe_truncated` as the canonical\ndead-publisher and truncation signals once this lands.\n\n### The fix, and the branch I did not take\n\nBoth alarms move to **`M = N = 6` over `period=300`** — a 1800s window, Profile A.\n\n**Keeping `breaching`.** I1 offers `notBreaching` as an alternative when an INTERMITTENT breach is\nthe real condition, and there is a sanctioned opt-out marker for it. I did not take it, because\nthe reasoning already recorded on this ticket settles the branch the other way: `ExecStopPost`\nfires on EVERY stop and a successful one publishes `probe_truncated = 0`, so a healthy period is a\npublished 0 and silence means a dead publisher. That is precisely the precondition `breaching`\nexists for. Taking the opt-out would undo the correction `make verify` forced on me earlier.\n\n**30 minutes, not the 900s I2 floor** — and for a reason specific to these two metrics rather than\nby copying the profile. `probe_ok` is emitted LAST in the script, so it gaps whenever a whole run\nis dropped, and the 25-minute head-of-script gap measured at 20:47Z is a **separate, still-open\ndefect** (`fragile-matronal-vole` / `a7bd-0cee-404f-4c06`) that drops runs outright. A 15-minute\nwindow here would page on that other bug. Revisit when `a7bd` closes and the cadence is\nre-measured — the measurement should set it, not the aspiration.\n\n**The cost, stated rather than hidden:** at `M = N` a single truncated run no longer pages. What\npages is 30 minutes of continuous truncation — which is the condition actually observed (55 kills\nagainst 197 runs in 24h, clustered), not a lone blip. A one-off truncation is still a published\ndatapoint and still visible on the metric.\n\n### State\n\nRebased onto current `origin/main` (now containing `a9d1` and `1bffbd58`). The failing test and its\nsibling suite pass locally (29), my own tests pass (43), lint clean. **Patchset 3 pushed to change\n2684**; the stale `Verified -1` was removed by the push.\n\nPlan-review attestation is **certified** and unaffected — `file_impact` is unchanged at the same\nfive paths and the description was not touched, so nothing staled it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788649131337931001,"uuid":"e829e8d2-ee71-4a5b-88e7-bcbf24e23615"}